rcq("R02-E-08-3", "Application / analogy", "Difficult",
    "Which of the following reasoning patterns is most similar to the one the author criticises at the end of paragraph 4?",
    "A forecaster explains each failed prediction, after the event, by pointing to local conditions that he had never said would matter.",
    [("A forecaster publicises the predictions that came true and quietly leaves out the many that did not when describing his record.",
      "This mirrors 'publishing successes while filing failures away' from paragraph 2, a different practice from appealing to unspecified moderators after a failure."),
     ("A forecaster revises his model after a failure and states in advance which conditions the revised model is meant to cover.",
      "Specifying conditions 'in advance so that it could be tested' is exactly what the author says the defenders' appeal lacks, so this is the remedy, not the fault."),
     ("A forecaster claims only that his method works for one coastal region and is consistently accurate within that region.",
      "This is an honest local claim that is borne out; the author's complaint is about general claims rescued by post hoc local excuses.")],
    "Paragraph 4 criticises 'an appeal to hidden moderators that is made only after a failure, and never specified in advance so that it could be tested'. A forecaster citing never-mentioned conditions after each miss reasons the same way, and so 'explains everything and therefore nothing'.",
    "Choosing the selective-reporting forecaster, whose fault is real but belongs to paragraph 2.",
    "Two distractors are close analogues of other ideas in the passage; the key must match the specific post hoc structure.",
    cognitive="Application", exams=HARD)

